# Line endings.
#
# `fly deploy` builds from the WORKING TREE, and on Windows that working tree
# is CRLF: core.autocrlf is on by default there, so a file committed as LF is
# checked out with CRLF and the Dockerfile copies it in that form.
#
# A shell script with CRLF line endings is not a shell script to Linux. The
# kernel reads the shebang as `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, looks for an interpreter
# literally named "bash\r", and the container dies at startup with
#
#   exec /app/ops/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# naming a file that is plainly there. The crontab is the same failure one
# level quieter: every line's last argument gains a \r, so the scrape writes
# to "/data/hkrd.db\r" — a second database, on the volume, that the dashboard
# never reads and nothing reports.
#
# So everything the container reads is pinned to LF regardless of platform.

* text=auto

*.sh            text eol=lf
ops/crontab     text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# Read by PowerShell on Windows and by nothing in the container. CRLF is what
# a Windows editor writes anyway, so pinning it keeps them out of every diff.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# The three double-click files, and the one place the rule runs the other way:
# a .bat with Unix line endings can mis-execute on Windows. They were written
# CRLF deliberately (ba31b61) and this is what keeps them that way in every
# checkout, on every platform, rather than leaving it to whoever cloned.
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Fixtures are compared byte for byte by the parser tests. Never touched.
tests/fixtures/*.html   -text
